            <Placemark id="2158">
                <name>
Historischer Kunstbunker
</name>
                <description>
During World War II, the Nazis repurposed this beer cellar to store stolen artwork.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/historischer-kunstbunker</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2157">
                    <coordinates>11.074888,49.45744,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2160">
                <name>
Henkerhaus
</name>
                <description>
Nestled on the banks of a river in Nuremberg is a small museum that was once home of the city's official executioner.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/henkerhaus</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2159">
                    <coordinates>11.073317,49.453289,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2162">
                <name>
Nuremberg Toy Museum
</name>
                <description>
This historic toy museum brings a little whimsy to a city not usually remembered for such things.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/nuremberg-toy-museum</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2161">
                    <coordinates>11.074437,49.454627,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2164">
                <name>
Ehekarussell
</name>
                <description>
This lurid German fountain plots the sad course of marriage from dating to death.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/ehekarussell</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2163">
                    <coordinates>11.070953,49.450553,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2166">
                <name>
The Kongresshalle, Nuremberg
</name>
                <description>
This abandoned coliseum was intended to be a grand Nazi rallying ground before it was halted at the end of the war.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/the-kongresshalle-nuremberg-nuremberg-germany</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2165">
                    <coordinates>11.111125,49.434143,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2168">
                <name>
Nuremberg Castle
</name>
                <description>
A castle from the Holy Roman Empire that hosts traveling youth... in its stables.
 &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/nuremberg-castle</description>
                <styleUrl>#42</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2167">
                    <coordinates>11.075834,49.45808,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
